1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,070
Last week, we started looking at reptiles, including crocodiles and snakes.

2
00:00:07,070 --> 00:00:10,720
Today, I'd like us to have a look at another reptile

3
00:00:11,080 --> 00:00:13,045
the lizard - and in particular,

4
00:00:13,045 --> 00:00:16,620
at some studies that have been done on a particular type of lizard

5
00:00:16,620 --> 00:00:20,140
whose Latin name is tiliqua rugosa.

6
00:00:20,140 --> 00:00:23,380
This is commonly known as the sleepy lizard

7
00:00:23,380 --> 00:00:25,337
because it's quite slow in its movements

8
00:00:25,337 --> 00:00:30,880
and spends quite a lot of its time dozing under rocks or lying in the sun.

9
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:33,840
I'll start with a general description.

10
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:39,260
Sleepy lizards live in Western and South Australia, where they're quite common.

11
00:00:39,260 --> 00:00:43,484
Unlike European lizards, which are mostly small, green and fast-moving,

12
00:00:43,484 --> 00:00:50,242
sleepy lizards are brown, but what's particularly distinctive about them is the colour of their tongue,

13
00:00:50,242 --> 00:00:57,000
which is dark blue, in contrast with the lining of their mouth which is bright pink.

14
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,410
And they're much bigger than most European lizards.

15
00:01:00,410 --> 00:01:03,344
They have quite a varied diet,

16
00:01:03,344 --> 00:01:05,740
including insects and even small animals

17
00:01:06,380 --> 00:01:10,190
but they mostly eat plants of varying kinds.

18
00:01:10,190 --> 00:01:12,870
Even though they're quite large and powerful,

19
00:01:12,870 --> 00:01:16,720
with strong jaws that can crush beetles and snail shells

20
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:19,380
they still have quite a few predators.

21
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:24,585
Large birds like cassowaries were one of the main ones in the past,

22
00:01:24,585 --> 00:01:29,390
but nowadays they're more likely to be caught and killed by snakes.

23
00:01:29,390 --> 00:01:35,031
Actually, another threat to their survival isn't a predator at all, but is man-made -

24
00:01:35,031 --> 00:01:39,740
quite a large number of sleepy lizards are killed by cars

25
00:01:39,740 --> 00:01:41,880
when they're trying to cross highways.

26
00:01:41,880 --> 00:01:49,910
One study carried out by Michael Freake at Flinders University investigated the methods of navigation of these lizards.

27
00:01:49,910 --> 00:01:54,840
Though they move slowly, they can travel quite long distances.

28
00:01:54,840 --> 00:01:56,700
And he found that even

29
00:01:56,700 --> 00:02:00,104
if they were taken some distance away from their home territory,

30
00:02:00,104 --> 00:02:05,369
they could usually find their way back home as long as they could see the sky -

31
00:02:05,369 --> 00:02:08,330
they didn't need any other landmarks on the ground.

32
00:02:08,330 --> 00:02:14,880
Observations of these lizards in the wild have also revealed that their mating habits are quite unusual.

33
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:19,131
Unlike most animals, it seems that they're relatively monogamous,

34
00:02:19,131 --> 00:02:22,910
returning to the same partner year after year.

35
00:02:22,910 --> 00:02:27,600
And the male and female also stay together for a long time

36
00:02:27,700 --> 00:02:31,050
both before and after the birth of their young.

37
00:02:31,050 --> 00:02:35,230
It's quite interesting to think about the possible reasons for this.

38
00:02:35,230 --> 00:02:38,915
It could be that it's to do with protecting their young -

39
00:02:38,915 --> 00:02:42,100
you'd expect them to have a much better chance of survival

40
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:44,610
if they have both parents around.

41
00:02:44,610 --> 00:02:50,310
But in fact observers have noted that once the babies have hatched out of their eggs,

42
00:02:50,310 --> 00:02:53,160
they have hardly any contact with their parents.

43
00:02:53,160 --> 00:02:56,950
So, there's not really any evidence to support that idea.

44
00:02:56,950 --> 00:03:03,761
Another suggestion's based on the observation that male lizards in monogamous relationships tend to be bigger

45
00:03:03,761 --> 00:03:05,890
and stronger than other males.

46
00:03:05,890 --> 00:03:08,680
So maybe the male lizards stay around

47
00:03:08,680 --> 00:03:12,610
so they can give the female lizards protection from other males.

48
00:03:12,610 --> 00:03:15,430
But again, we're not really sure.

49
00:03:15,430 --> 00:03:21,820
Finally, I'd like to mention another study that involved collecting data by tracking the lizards.

50
00:03:21,820 --> 00:03:24,990
I was actually involved in this myself.

51
00:03:24,990 --> 00:03:27,560
So we caught some lizards in the wild

52
00:03:27,560 --> 00:03:32,379
and we developed a tiny GPS system that would allow us to track them,

53
00:03:32,379 --> 00:03:34,730
and we fixed this onto their tails.

54
00:03:34,730 --> 00:03:37,460
Then we set the lizards free again

55
00:03:37,460 --> 00:03:41,636
and we were able to track them for twelve days and gather data,

56
00:03:41,636 --> 00:03:43,220
not just about their location

57
00:03:43,220 --> 00:03:47,160
but even about how many steps they took during this period.

58
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:54,304
One surprising thing we discovered from this is that there were far fewer meetings between lizards than we expected -

59
00:03:54,304 --> 00:03:58,440
it seems that they were actually trying to avoid one another.

60
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:00,700
So why would that be?

61
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:03,623
Well, again we have no clear evidence,

62
00:04:03,623 --> 00:04:09,886
but one hypothesis is that male lizards can cause quite serious injuries to one another,

63
00:04:09,886 --> 00:04:12,860
so maybe this avoidance is a way of preventing this

64
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:16,150
of self-preservation, if you like.

65
00:04:16,150 --> 00:04:20,740
But we need to collect a lot more data before we can be sure of any of this.
